; ACE Startup-Sequence
;
; The second half of an Amiga boot, and the half that is not privileged.
; dos.library establishes SYS: and the drawers under it -- C:, S:, L:, LIBS:,
; DEVS:, FONTS: -- before any shell exists, because a shell cannot find this
; script, or the Assign that would make an assign, until they are there. ACE's
; broker does that same job, in rom/dos/cliinit.c's order and with its
; fallbacks. Everything from this point on is ordinary commands, run by the
; ordinary shell, and this file is meant to be read and changed.
;
; Two of AROS's own assigns are made by the broker here rather than by this
; script. AROS says
;
;     Assign "T:"   "RAM:T"
;     Assign "ENV:" "RAM:ENV"
;
; because on an Amiga the RAM disk is a fixed part of the system and a script
; can name it. ACE names the host's tmpfs mounts RAM:, RAM1: ... in the order
; the host happens to have mounted them, so which one is "the" RAM disk is an
; accident of the machine rather than something a script could rely on. They
; point at the host's own per-user runtime directory instead: tmpfs, private,
; and emptied between boots, which is what RAM: was being asked for.
;
; Not here: the Mount/Automount/SetPatch/IPrefs half of a real boot, none of
; which ACE has anything to mount, patch or prefer. Copy's ENVARC: to ENV:
; step is performed by the broker during boot, and Path is available to the
; scripts that need to extend command lookup.

FailAt 21

; Where a user's own startup belongs. Anything in here is overwritten by the
; next install; S:User-Startup is not, which is the whole point of it.
If EXISTS S:User-Startup
Execute S:User-Startup
EndIf
